WORKER.JS README
San Juan Coastline blog: the Cloudflare Worker behind blog/admin.html
=====================================================================

WHAT IT IS
  worker.js is a small program that runs on Cloudflare (Worker name: rollaword-auth).
  It is NOT stored in the GitHub repo. To change it: Cloudflare dashboard > the Worker >
  Edit code > paste the new worker.js > Deploy.

  The admin page (blog/admin.html + admin.js) never talks to GitHub. It only talks to this Worker.
  (You can still change the repository yourself any time with GitHub Desktop. The Worker is how
  the family's posts get in without them needing GitHub.)


THE THREE THINGS IT DOES
------------------------

1. CHECKS WHO IS SIGNING IN
   - Every request from the admin page carries the person's Google sign-in.
   - The Worker asks Google to confirm it is genuine, then checks that the Gmail address is on the
     approved list (ALLOWED_EMAILS).
   - Anyone else is refused. Nothing is read or saved for them.

2. SAVES THE FAMILY'S POSTS TO GITHUB FOR THEM
   - The family (and the admin page) have no GitHub access at all. When they save a post, the
     Worker writes it to the repository on their behalf.
   - The Worker holds the GitHub token (GITHUB_PAT). The family's browsers never see it, so the
     only ways into the repository are: you, with GitHub Desktop, and this Worker, which acts only
     for people on the approved list.
   - It only writes inside the blog folder.
   - It saves everything for one action in ONE commit, so the site deploys once.
   - If two people save at the same moment, it starts again from the newest version, so one
     save can never overwrite a newer one.

3. BUILDS THE PAGES
   - When a post is saved, the Worker creates the finished HTML from blog/template.html:
       blog/posts/<name>.html   the post page (with its Coastline / Farmland chip)
       the Previous / Next links on the neighbouring posts
       blog/index.html          the blog list, with the post count
       blog/sitemap.xml         the blog sitemap
       blog/posts.json          the list of posts (what the editor reads)
   - It only writes the MARKUP (the page structure and text). It does NOT contain any styling
     or filter script (see below).


WHAT IT DOES NOT DO ANY MORE: LOOK AND FILTER
---------------------------------------------
   blog/blog.css    how the post list and the Coastline / Farmland chips look
   blog/filter.js   how the Section filter on the index behaves

   These are ordinary files in the repo. Edit them in VSCodium, preview with Live Server, commit
   and push in GitHub Desktop. No Cloudflare change and no Rebuild is needed.
   The Worker only adds two lines to the pages that link to them.


WHAT THE ADMIN PAGE CAN ASK THE WORKER TO DO
--------------------------------------------
   list      the posts for the admin list (title, date, draft, sections)
   get       one post, to open it in the editor
   save      create or update a post (does job 3, in one commit)
   delete    remove a post and rebuild the neighbours, index and sitemap
   rebuild   rewrite every page from blog/template.html (25 posts at a time)
   upload    store a photo in blog/images/


SECTIONS (TAGS)
---------------
   - There are exactly two: coastline and farmland. A post has one or both.
   - A post with no section saved yet counts as coastline.
   - The editor will not save a post with neither selected.
   - Sections are stored in blog/posts.json and shown as a chip on each post page and as a label
     in the index list.


GOOGLE ANALYTICS (TAG ID: GTM-5XJ53P8H)
---------------------------------------
   Added 2026-11-21.

   WHAT IT IS
     Google Analytics counts visitors, page views, countries, devices and where people came from.
     The tag ID is GTM-5XJ53P8H. Google generated it for the Analytics data stream.
     THE REPORT TO USE: the Analytics property named "rollaword" (property ID 565269591).
     Do NOT use the property named "rollaword.com" (property ID 565324386). It was created by
     accident, receives nothing, and can be ignored (or moved to the Trash Can some day under
     Admin > Property settings).

     URL of the live report (Realtime, rollaword property):
       https://analytics.google.com/analytics/web/#/a183877144p565269591/realtime/pages
     Bookmark it. If Analytics opens a different property, click the property name at the top
     left and choose "rollaword" (not "rollaword.com").
     For the full reports, use the left menu on the same page: Reports > Reports snapshot (they
     take 24 to 48 hours to fill in).

   THE SNIPPET
     It goes inside <head>, straight after the AdSense line:

       <script async src="https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtm.js?id=GTM-5XJ53P8H"></script>
       <script>window.dataLayer=window.dataLayer||[];function gtag(){dataLayer.push(arguments);}gtag('js',new Date());</script>

   WHERE IT LIVES
     blog/template.html   after the AdSense line in <head>. This covers every post. The Worker
                          reads this file when it builds pages, so worker.js was NOT changed and
                          nothing needs pasting into Cloudflare. After editing the template,
                          press Rebuild all pages in blog/admin.html and the snippet reaches
                          every existing post.
     blog/index.html      built by the Worker. The snippet is in the copy that was added by hand.
                          After a Rebuild, check View page source for GTM-5XJ53P8H. If it has
                          disappeared, the index head built by worker.js needs the snippet too.
     blog/post.html       a hand-written page (loads posts in the browser). The snippet was added
                          by hand, after the AdSense line. Edit it in VSCodium, commit and push.
     blog/admin.html      deliberately NOT tracked. It is a private editing page (noindex), and
                          tracking it would count the family's editing as visitors.
     The rest of rollaword.com (the Publii pages)
                          Publii > Tools & Plugins > Custom HTML > Head. Publii adds it to every
                          page it makes. Re-render and sync (or commit and push) to publish.

   BACKFILLING OLD POSTS (only if Rebuild all pages does not do it)
     add-analytics.html is a one-time browser tool. It adds the snippet after <head> in every
     .html file in a folder you choose, and skips files that already have it.
       - Run it on your own computer, after pulling the latest posts in GitHub Desktop.
       - Do NOT run it through Live Server: Live Server reloads the page on the first file change
         and stops the run part way. Open it from disk (double-click), or add
         "liveServer.settings.ignoreFiles": ["blog/posts/**"] to the VSCodium settings.
       - Do a dry run first, then untick Dry run and run it again.
       - Keep the tool out of the project folder you push, so it does not end up on the live site.

   HOW TO CHECK IT IS WORKING
     1. Open a live page, choose View page source, and search for GTM-5XJ53P8H.
     2. Keep the page open, then look at Analytics > Reports > Realtime. A visit shows up within
        about a minute.
     3. Ad blockers and privacy extensions stop the tag. Test in a private window or on a phone
        using mobile data.
     Visits from Live Server previews may also be counted.


SETTINGS ON CLOUDFLARE (Worker > Settings > Variables and Secrets)
------------------------------------------------------------------
   GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID   your Google OAuth client ID
   ALLOWED_EMAILS     approved Gmail addresses, separated by commas or semicolons
   GITHUB_PAT         (Secret) fine-grained token, this repo only, Contents: read and write
   Only these addresses may use the Worker: https://rollaword.com and https://www.rollaword.com


WHAT TO CHANGE, AND WHERE
-------------------------
   I want to change...                                Edit                      Then
   -------------------------------------------------  ------------------------  ---------------------
   how the chips / list look                          blog/blog.css             commit + push
   how the Section filter behaves                     blog/filter.js            commit + push
   the page frame (menu, header, footer, AdSense,     blog/template.html        commit + push, then
   Google Analytics)                                                            press Rebuild all pages
   Google Analytics on the single-post page           blog/post.html            commit + push
   the editor screen                                  blog/admin.html,          commit + push
                                                      admin.js, admin.css
   what the pages contain / how they are built,       worker.js                 paste into Cloudflare,
   the sign-in list rules, the sections               (on Cloudflare)           deploy, then press
                                                                                Rebuild all pages
   who may sign in                                    ALLOWED_EMAILS            save in Cloudflare


BLOG COMMENTS (SUPABASE)
------------------------
   Added 2026-11-23. Work in progress: reader comments, every comment held for approval.

   HOW IT WORKS
     - Comments are stored in a free Supabase project (not on GitHub, not on Cloudflare).
     - Every new comment starts as NOT approved. Only approved comments are shown to the public.
     - The family approves or deletes comments on blog/blogApproval.html (a private page,
       noindex, no AdSense, no Analytics). Sign in with the Google account.
     - Only the three family Gmail addresses listed in blog-comments-setup.sql may approve or
       delete. The database enforces this, not the page.
     - Flat comments only (no replies). No notification email yet.

   FILES
     blog/blogApproval.html       the approval page (Waiting tab, Approved tab)
     blog-comments-setup.sql      run ONCE in the Supabase SQL Editor. Keep it OUT of the folder
                                  you push to the live site. It holds the family email list.

   SUPABASE SETTINGS TO REMEMBER
     Project ID             knuatcllwictmybubjys
     Project URL            https://knuatcllwictmybubjys.supabase.co
     Public (publishable) key is in blogApproval.html. It is meant to be public.
     Google client          the SAME Google OAuth client as the blog editor (GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID in
                            Cloudflare). Supabase also needs that client's SECRET, which is kept
                            only inside Supabase and is NOT written in this file.
     Never put the Supabase "service_role" / "secret" key in any page.

   STILL TO DO
     - Run blog-comments-setup.sql (with the three real Gmail addresses).
     - Save the Google provider in Supabase (client ID + secret), then test the sign-in.
     - Add the redirect URLs listed below under Supabase URL Configuration.
     - Build the reader comment box on the posts (anonymous or Google/Microsoft sign-in,
       Cloudflare Turnstile, length limit, daily cap).
     - Later, when understood: delete the extra Google client secret (two exist for now).


